1. project overview and goal
1.1. dynamic crowd modelling objectives
The modern urban transport infrastructure of Vienna requires proactive municipal management systems capable of mitigating pedestrian congestion and bicycle lane bottlenecks before they trigger safety hazards or transit delays. The goal of this big data project is to construct an end-to-end distributed data processing pipeline that ingests high-frequency wearable telemetry, scales these signals into a statistically representative synthetic population, maps their trajectories onto physical city infrastructure, and forecasts localized saturation levels. Rather than relying on standard macro-level static counts, this system models urban movement as a fluid, high-velocity stream of individual kinetic footprints. By predicting spatial-temporal saturation indices 30 to 60 minutes in advance, the pipeline provides public transit authorities and urban planners with the predictive insights necessary to optimize transit routing and manage city space dynamically
